window.docContents[140].resolve({"/Basic-Types/Arrays/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Basic-Types--Arrays--API-Reference--Transformation":{"contents":"Applies a function to each element of the array, returning the resulting array of values.Examples:* #[a, b, c].map f = #[f a, f b, f c]* #[].map Nat.succ = #[]* #[\"one\", \"two\", \"three\"].map (·.length) = #[3, 3, 5]* #[\"one\", \"two\", \"three\"].map (·.reverse) = #[\"eno\", \"owt\", \"eerht\"]\n\nApplies a function to each element of an array, returning the array of results. The function is\nmonomorphic: it is required to return a value of the same type. The internal implementation uses\npointer equality, and does not allocate a new array if the result of each function call is\npointer-equal to its argument.\n\nApplies the monadic action f to every element in the array, left-to-right, and returns the array\nof results.\n\nApplies the monadic action f to every element in the array, left-to-right, and returns the array\nof results. Furthermore, the resulting array's type guarantees that it contains the same number of\nelements as the input array.\n\nApplies a monadic function to each element of an array, returning the array of results. The function is\nmonomorphic: it is required to return a value of the same type. The internal implementation uses\npointer equality, and does not allocate a new array if the result of each function call is\npointer-equal to its argument.\n\nApplies a function to each element of the array along with the index at which that element is found,\nreturning the array of results.Array.mapFinIdx is a variant that additionally provides the function with a proof that the index\nis valid.\n\nApplies the monadic action f to every element in the array, along with the element's index, from\nleft to right. Returns the array of results.\n\nApplies a function to each element of the array along with the index at which that element is found,\nreturning the array of results. In addition to the index, the function is also provided with a proof\nthat the index is valid.Array.mapIdx is a variant that does not provide the function with evidence that the index is\nvalid.\n\nApplies the monadic action f to every element in the array, along with the element's index and a\nproof that the index is in bounds, from left to right. Returns the array of results.\n\nApplies a function that returns an array to each element of an array. The resulting arrays are\nappended.Examples:* #[2, 3, 2].flatMap Array.range = #[0, 1, 0, 1, 2, 0, 1]* #[['a', 'b'], ['c', 'd', 'e']].flatMap List.toArray = #['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']\n\nApplies a monadic function that returns an array to each element of an array, from left to right.\nThe resulting arrays are appended.\n\nCombines two arrays into an array of pairs in which the first and second components are the\ncorresponding elements of each input array. The resulting array is the length of the shorter of the\ninput arrays.Examples:* #[\"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\"].zip #[1, 2, 3] = #[(\"Mon\", 1), (\"Tue\", 2), (\"Wed\", 3)]* #[\"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\"].zip #[1, 2] = #[(\"Mon\", 1), (\"Tue\", 2)]* #[x₁, x₂, x₃].zip #[y₁, y₂, y₃, y₄] = #[(x₁, y₁), (x₂, y₂), (x₃, y₃)]\n\nApplies a function to the corresponding elements of two arrays, stopping at the end of the shorter\narray.Examples:* #[1, 2].zipWith (· + ·) #[5, 6] = #[6, 8]* #[1, 2, 3].zipWith (· + ·) #[5, 6, 10] = #[6, 8, 13]* #[].zipWith (· + ·) #[5, 6] = #[]* #[x₁, x₂, x₃].zipWith f #[y₁, y₂, y₃, y₄] = #[f x₁ y₁, f x₂ y₂, f x₃ y₃]\n\nApplies a function to the corresponding elements of both arrays, stopping when there are no more\nelements in either array. If one array is shorter than the other, the function is passed none for\nthe missing elements.Examples:* #[1, 6].zipWithAll min #[5, 2] = #[some 1, some 2]* #[1, 2, 3].zipWithAll Prod.mk #[5, 6] = #[(some 1, some 5), (some 2, some 6), (some 3, none)]* #[x₁, x₂].zipWithAll f #[y] = #[f (some x₁) (some y), f (some x₂) none]\n\nPairs each element of an array with its index, optionally starting from an index other than 0.Examples:* #[a, b, c].zipIdx = #[(a, 0), (b, 1), (c, 2)]* #[a, b, c].zipIdx 5 = #[(a, 5), (b, 6), (c, 7)]\n\nSeparates an array of pairs into two arrays that contain the respective first and second components.Examples:* #[(\"Monday\", 1), (\"Tuesday\", 2)].unzip = (#[\"Monday\", \"Tuesday\"], #[1, 2])* #[(x₁, y₁), (x₂, y₂), (x₃, y₃)].unzip = (#[x₁, x₂, x₃], #[y₁, y₂, y₃])* (#[] : Array (Nat × String)).unzip = ((#[], #[]) : List Nat × List String)\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Basic Types\u0009Arrays\u0009API Reference","header":"21.16.4.9. Transformation","id":"/Basic-Types/Arrays/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Basic-Types--Arrays--API-Reference--Transformation"},"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Basic-Types--Maps-and-Sets--Extensional-Hash-Maps--Queries":{"contents":"Returns true if there is a mapping for the given key. There is also a Prop-valued version\nof this: a ∈ m is equivalent to m.contains a = true.Observe that this is different behavior than for lists: for lists, ∈ uses = and contains uses\n== for comparisons, while for hash maps, both use ==.\n\nThe notation m[a] or m[a]'h is preferred over calling this function directly.Retrieves the mapping for the given key. Ensures that such a mapping exists by requiring a proof of\na ∈ m.\n\nThe notation m[a]! is preferred over calling this function directly.Tries to retrieve the mapping for the given key, panicking if no such mapping is present.\n\nThe notation m[a]? is preferred over calling this function directly.Tries to retrieve the mapping for the given key, returning none if no such mapping is present.\n\nTries to retrieve the mapping for the given key, returning fallback if no such mapping is present.\n\nRetrieves the key from the mapping that matches a. Ensures that such a mapping exists by\nrequiring a proof of a ∈ m. The result is guaranteed to be pointer equal to the key in the map.\n\nChecks if a mapping for the given key exists and returns the key if it does, otherwise panics.\nIf no panic occurs the result is guaranteed to be pointer equal to the key in the map.\n\nChecks if a mapping for the given key exists and returns the key if it does, otherwise none.\nThe result in the some case is guaranteed to be pointer equal to the key in the map.\n\nChecks if a mapping for the given key exists and returns the key if it does, otherwise fallback.\nIf a mapping exists the result is guaranteed to be pointer equal to the key in the map.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Basic Types\u0009Maps and Sets\u0009Extensional Hash Maps","header":"21.19.4.3. Queries","id":"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Basic-Types--Maps-and-Sets--Extensional-Hash-Maps--Queries"},"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Package-Configuration-File-Format--Lean-Format--Cache-Services":{"contents":"Cache services are declared using the cache_service declaration.\nThe declaration's body should be one of three smart constructors, downloadService, downloadArtsService, or reservoirService.\n\nCache Service DeclarationsDefine a cache service for the package.cache_service «service-name» :=\n  /- term of type `CacheService` -/\n\n\nConstructs a CacheService to download artifacts and/or outputs from an S3 endpoint.\n\nConstructs a CacheService to download just artifacts from an S3 endpoint.\n\nConstructs a CacheService for a Reservoir endpoint.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Package Configuration File Format\u0009Lean Format","header":"25.1.7.2.6. Cache Services","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Package-Configuration-File-Format--Lean-Format--Cache-Services"},"/Interacting-with-Lean/#hash-guard_msgs":{"contents":"The #guard_msgs command can be used to ensure that the messages output by a command are as expected.\nTogether with the interaction commands in this section, it can be used to construct a file that will only elaborate if the output is as expected; such a file can be used as a test driver in Lake.\n\nDocumenting Expected Output/-- ... -/ #guard_msgs in cmd captures the messages generated by the command cmd\nand checks that they match the contents of the docstring.Basic example:/--\nerror: Unknown identifier `x`\n-/\n#guard_msgs in\nexample : α := x\nThis checks that there is such an error and then consumes the message.By default, the command captures all messages, but the filter condition can be adjusted.\nFor example, we can select only warnings:/--\nwarning: declaration uses 'sorry'\n-/\n#guard_msgs(warning) in\nexample : α := sorry\nor only errors#guard_msgs(error) in\nexample : α := sorry\nIn the previous example, since warnings are not captured there is a warning on sorry.\nWe can drop the warning completely with#guard_msgs(error, drop warning) in\nexample : α := sorry\nIn general, #guard_msgs accepts a comma-separated list of configuration clauses in parentheses:#guard_msgs (configElt,*) in cmd\nBy default, the configuration list is\n(check all, whitespace := normalized, ordering := exact, positions := false).Message filters select messages by severity:* info, warning, error: (non-trace) messages with the given severity level.* trace: trace messages* all: all messages.The filters can be prefixed with the action to take:* check (the default): capture and check the message* drop: drop the message* pass: let the message pass throughIf no filter is specified, check all is assumed.  Otherwise, these filters are processed in\nleft-to-right order, with an implicit pass all at the end.Whitespace handling (after trimming leading and trailing whitespace):* whitespace := exact requires an exact whitespace match.* whitespace := normalized converts all newline characters to a space before matching\n(the default). This allows breaking long lines.* whitespace := lax collapses whitespace to a single space before matching.Message ordering:* ordering := exact uses the exact ordering of the messages (the default).* ordering := sorted sorts the messages in lexicographic order.\nThis helps with testing commands that are non-deterministic in their ordering.Position reporting:* positions := true reports the ranges of all messages relative to the line on which\n#guard_msgs appears.* positions := false does not report position info.Substring matching:* substring := true checks that the docstring appears as a substring of the output\n(after whitespace normalization). This is useful when you only care about part of the message.* substring := false (the default) requires exact matching (modulo whitespace normalization).Stabilizing output:\nWhen messages contain autogenerated names (e.g., metavariables like ?m.47), the output may\ndiffer between runs or Lean versions. Use set_option pp.mvars.anonymous false to replace\nanonymous metavariables with ?_ while preserving user-named metavariables like ?a.\nAlternatively, set_option pp.mvars false replaces all metavariables with ?_.\nSimilarly, set_option pp.fvars.anonymous false replaces loose free variable names like\n_fvar.22 with _fvar._.For example, #guard_msgs (error, drop all) in cmd means to check errors and drop\neverything else.The command elaborator has special support for #guard_msgs for linting.\nThe #guard_msgs itself wants to capture linter warnings,\nso it elaborates the command it is attached to as if it were a top-level command.\nHowever, the command elaborator runs linters for all top-level commands,\nwhich would include #guard_msgs itself, and would cause duplicate and/or uncaptured linter warnings.\nThe top-level command elaborator only runs the linters if #guard_msgs is not present.\n\nTesting Return ValuesThe #guard_msgs command can ensure that a set of test cases pass:def reverse : List α → List α := helper []\nwhere\n  helper acc\n    | [] => acc\n    | x :: xs => helper (x :: acc) xs\n\n/-- info: [] -/\n#guard_msgs in\n#eval reverse ([] : List Nat)\n\n/-- info: ['c', 'b', 'a'] -/\n#guard_msgs in\n#eval reverse \"abc\".toList\n\n\nThe behavior of the #guard_msgs command can be specified in three ways:1. Providing a filter that selects a subset of messages to be checked2. Specifying a whitespace comparison strategy3. Deciding to sort messages by their content or by the order in which they were producedThese configuration options are provided in parentheses, separated by commas.\n\nSpecifying #guard_msgs BehaviorThere are three kinds of options for #guard_msgs: filters, whitespace comparison strategies, and orderings.\n\nOutput Filters for #guard_msgsA message filter specification for #guard_msgs.* info, warning, error: capture (non-trace) messages with the given severity level.* trace: captures trace messages* all: capture all messages.The filters can be prefixed with* check (the default): capture and check the message* drop: drop the message* pass: let the message pass throughIf no filter is specified, check all is assumed.  Otherwise, these filters are processed in\nleft-to-right order, with an implicit pass all at the end.\n\nWhitespace Comparison for #guard_msgsLeading and trailing whitespace is always ignored when comparing messages. On top of that, the following settings are available:* whitespace := exact requires an exact whitespace match.* whitespace := normalized converts all newline characters to a space before matching (the default). This allows breaking long lines.* whitespace := lax collapses whitespace to a single space before matching.\n\nThe option guard_msgs.diff controls the content of the error message that #guard_msgs produces when the expected message doesn't match the produced message.\nBy default, #guard_msgs shows a line-by-line difference, with a leading + used to indicate lines from the produced message and a leading - used to indicate lines from the expected message.\nWhen messages are large and only differ by a small amount, this can make it easier to notice where they differ.\nSetting guard_msgs.diff to false causes #guard_msgs to instead show just the produced message, which can be compared with the expected message in the source file.\nThis can be convenient if the difference between the message is confusing or overwhelming.\n\nWhen true, show a diff between expected and actual messages if they don't match.\n\nDisplaying DifferencesThe #guard_msgs command can be used to test definition of a rose tree Tree and a function Tree.big that creates them:inductive Tree (α : Type u) : Type u where\n  | val : α → Tree α\n  | branches : List (Tree α) → Tree α\n\ndef Tree.big (n : Nat) : Tree Nat :=\n  if n < 5 then .branches [.val n, .val (n - 1), .val n, .val (n - 2)]\n  else .branches [.big (n / 2),  .big (n / 3)]\nHowever, it can be difficult to spot where test failures come from when the output is large:set_option guard_msgs.diff false\n/--\ninfo: Tree.branches\n  [Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches\n        [Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0],\n         Tree.branches [Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1]],\n   Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0]]]\n-/\n#guard_msgs in\n#eval Tree.big 20\nThe evaluation produces:Tree.branches\n  [Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches\n        [Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0],\n         Tree.branches [Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0]],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1]],\n   Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0]]]\nWithout guard_msgs.diff, the #guard_msgs command reports this error:❌️ Docstring on `#guard_msgs` does not match generated message:\n\ninfo: Tree.branches\n  [Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches\n        [Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0],\n         Tree.branches [Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0]],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1]],\n   Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0]]]\nEnabling guard_msgs.diff highlights the differences instead, making the error more apparent:set_option guard_msgs.diff true in\n/--\ninfo: Tree.branches\n  [Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches\n        [Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0],\n         Tree.branches [Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0,\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1]],\n   Tree.branches\n     [Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1],\n      Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0]]]\n-/\n#guard_msgs in\n#eval Tree.big 20\n❌️ Docstring on `#guard_msgs` does not match generated message:\n\n  info: Tree.branches\n    [Tree.branches\n       [Tree.branches\n          [Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0],\n-          Tree.branches [Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0,\n+          Tree.branches [Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 0]],\n        Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1]],\n     Tree.branches\n       [Tree.branches [Tree.val 3, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 3, Tree.val 1],\n        Tree.branches [Tree.val 2, Tree.val 1, Tree.val 2, Tree.val 0]]]\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Interacting with Lean","header":"3.6. Testing Output with  #guard_msgs","id":"/Interacting-with-Lean/#hash-guard_msgs"},"/The-Type-System/Quotients/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--The-Type-System--Quotients--Logical-Model--Low-Level-Quotient-API":{"contents":"Quot.liftOn is an version of Quot.lift that takes the quotient type's value first, by analogy to Quotient.liftOn.\n\nLifts a function from an underlying type to a function on a quotient, requiring that it respects the\nquotient's relation.Given a relation r : α → α → Prop and a quotient's value q : Quot r, applying a f : α → β\nrequires a proof c that f respects r. In this case, Quot.liftOn q f h : β evaluates\nto the result of applying f to the underlying value in α from q.Quot.liftOn is a version of the built-in primitive Quot.lift with its parameters re-ordered.Quotient types are described in more detail in the Lean Language\nReference.\n\nLean also provides convenient elimination from Quot into any subsingleton without further proof obligations, along with dependent elimination principles that correspond to those used for Quotient.\n\nAn alternative induction principle for quotients that can be used when the target type is a\nsubsingleton, in which all elements are equal.In these cases, the proof that the function respects the quotient's relation is trivial, so any\nfunction can be lifted.Quot.rec does not assume that the type is a subsingleton.\n\nA dependent recursion principle for Quot. It is analogous to the\nrecursor for a structure, and can be used when the resulting type\nis not necessarily a proposition.While it is very general, this recursor can be tricky to use. The following simpler alternatives may\nbe easier to use:* Quot.lift is useful for defining non-dependent functions.* Quot.ind is useful for proving theorems about quotients.* Quot.recOnSubsingleton can be used whenever the target type is a Subsingleton.* Quot.hrecOn uses heterogeneous equality instead of rewriting with\nQuot.sound.Quot.recOn is a version of this recursor that takes the quotient parameter first.\n\nA dependent recursion principle for Quot that takes the quotient first. It is analogous to the\nrecursor for a structure, and can be used when the resulting type\nis not necessarily a proposition.While it is very general, this recursor can be tricky to use. The following simpler alternatives may\nbe easier to use:* Quot.lift is useful for defining non-dependent functions.* Quot.ind is useful for proving theorems about quotients.* Quot.recOnSubsingleton can be used whenever the target type is a Subsingleton.* Quot.hrecOn uses heterogeneous equality instead of rewriting with\nQuot.sound.Quot.rec is a version of this recursor that takes the quotient parameter last.\n\nA dependent recursion principle for Quot that uses heterogeneous\nequality, analogous to a recursor for\na structure.Quot.recOn is a version of this recursor that uses Eq instead of HEq.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Type System\u0009Quotients\u0009Logical Model","header":"4.5.5.3. Low-Level Quotient API","id":"/The-Type-System/Quotients/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--The-Type-System--Quotients--Logical-Model--Low-Level-Quotient-API"},"/releases/v4.34.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___34___0-_LPAR_2026-09-14_RPAR_--Documentation":{"contents":"* #14436\nremoves references to the unfolding lemma from the repeatM docstrings and moves that lemma into the repeatM.Internal namespace.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.34.0 (2026-09-14)","header":"Documentation","id":"/releases/v4.34.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___34___0-_LPAR_2026-09-14_RPAR_--Documentation"},"/releases/v4.37.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___37___0-rc1-_LPAR_2026-11-18_RPAR_--Language":{"contents":"* #16533\nchanges registerReservedNamePredicate to take two functions: a test on the last component of the name, and the predicate itself. A name is a candidate for being reserved only if its last component passes the test.* #16531\nfixes an \"environment already contains\" import error when two modules realize the same match congruence equation.* #16512\nfixes a bug wherein loose bvars where not always checked for in inductive declarations. This issue looks benign, and does not lead to an apparent proof of false.* #16519\nmakes the generation of match equations faster for a match with many alternatives and a catch-all. The big_match_string benchmark with 128 alternatives is about 14% faster (#16313).* #16505\nmakes each congruence equation f.match_1.congr_eq_i of a match be generated on demand, independently of the other ones. It fixes a performance regression from #16502 for a match with many alternatives where only a few match equations are not proved by rfl, for example literals or constructors followed by a catch-all: big_match_nat_split and big_match_partial are 18% and 7% faster. Referring to f.match_1.congr_eq_i by name also generates only that theorem.* #16503\nmakes the kernel type check a constant created by a nested realization only once. For example, the equation theorems of a function request the equations of its match, and the kernel checked each of those theorems twice, or three times when they requested further theorems themselves. Generating the equations of a String → Nat function with 128 literal alternatives is about 25% faster. It also fixes repeated error messages: a kernel error in such a constant was reported once per nesting level, and is now reported once.* #16501\nadds the option shiftLeft.threshold (default: 65536). whnf, simp, and grind no longer evaluate a left shift of literals when the shift amount is above this threshold, and report a warning instead, as they already do for exponents above exponentiation.threshold. Previously, a shift amount of 2^32 or more aborted the Lean process with INTERNAL PANIC: Nat.shiftl exponent is too big, e.g., in example : (1 <<< 4294967296 : Nat) = 0 := by simp. cbv and the compiler use the same bound as a constant.* #16404\nmakes the do elaborator reject a finally block that reassigns a mut variable in scope at the try. The error names the reassigned variables:A `finally` block cannot reassign the `mut` variable `s`.\n* #16482\nturns the csimpCore test, which checks that declarations replaced by csimp are noncomputable, into a linter.* #16480\nmakes equation theorems of nonrecursive definitions faster to type check when the definition is a match on literals. For a String → Nat function with 128 String literal alternatives, generating its equation theorems, which simp [f] and cbv need, takes 15.5s instead of 25.7s.* #15856\nadds an optional starting position to Lean.Parser.parseHeader, allowing tools such as Verso to parse module headers inside a larger source file while preserving source locations.* #16477\nmakes generating the equations of a match faster when a discriminant is annotated with h : and the patterns are values. For match h : s with and 64 String literal alternatives, it takes 0.40s instead of 1.50s.* #16476\nmakes the proofs that two String literals are different cheaper to check, improving on #16474. For two literals that share a prefix of 512 characters, the kernel checks the proof in 3.5ms instead of 4.4ms.* #16474\nmakes the proofs that two String literals are different faster to build and to check. These proofs are used by simp, cbv, and the equations of match expressions with String patterns. For two literals that share a prefix of 64 characters, the kernel checks the proof in 0.60ms instead of 1.85ms.* #16465\nmakes generating the equations of a match with many value patterns faster. For a match with two discriminants and 64 alternatives such as | \"name\", 0 => .. followed by a catch-all, it takes 0.38s instead of 1.16s.* #16460\nmakes generating the equations of a match with many alternatives faster when the patterns contain literals nested in constructors, e.g., | some \"a\" => ... For 32 alternatives of this form, generating the match equations takes 0.53s instead of 1.45s.* #16354\nspeeds up the generation of equation lemmas for definitions that match on String literals. With 64 literal patterns, generation used to hit the heartbeat limit.* #16408\nmakes elaborating large mutual blocks defined by well-founded recursion faster. Cleaning up the termination proof goals no longer takes time proportional to the number of functions in the block, which used to make elaborating the whole block quadratic. As a side effect, the functional induction principle of a function with a nested recursive call, such as f (f x), no longer contains a duplicate induction hypothesis for the inner call; proofs that name the hypotheses of such a case positionally need to drop one name.* #16422\nsimplifies the result type of the function that packs a mutual block defined by well-founded recursion: where both branches of a case split on the packed argument have the same non-dependent result type, the case split is omitted. If all functions have the same result type, the packed function now has that type, as the docstring of ArgsPacker.Mutual.uncurryType already promised. Previously this type was a case split over all functions of the block, which appeared in the motive of every case split in the definition and its unfolding lemma and made type checking large blocks slow.* #16409\npacks the arguments of a mutual block defined by well-founded recursion into a balanced tree of PSum types instead of a right-nested chain. The nesting depth of packed arguments and of the case splits that take them apart becomes logarithmic in the number of functions, which makes elaborating and type checking large blocks much cheaper. Blocks of up to three functions are packed as before.* #16320\nfixes a segfault when withImportModules is called a second time in one process and the imported module imports another package root, a regression from #15291.* #16279\nfixes Expr.hasLooseBVar, Expr.liftLooseBVars, Expr.lowerLooseBVars, task priorities and the kernel's handling of the number of parameters of an inductive declaration, which silently truncated values of 2^32 and above to 32 bits. For example, the kernel accepted an inductive declaration with numParams := 2^32 and recorded numParams = 0, and a task with priority 2^32 - 1 ran synchronously in the caller rather than on a dedicated thread.* #15889\nmakes type class resolution reuse cached results much more often. A result is shared by all local contexts that agree on the local instances of the classes its search consulted, and declaring or erasing an instance only invalidates the results whose search consulted the instance's class. Building all of Mathlib takes about 2.7% less wall-clock time, and about 6.7% less time in type class resolution.* #16273\nmakes set_option maxRecDepth 0 mean \"no limit\" in the compiler and in macro expansion, and makes huge maxRecDepth values act as unlimited in the kernel instead of wrapping around to a small limit. Previously, maxRecDepth 0 was stricter than e.g. maxRecDepth 10000, and values such as 2^60 could reject shallow declarations.* #16235\nmakes the equation lemmas f.eq_<i> keep the haves and lets of the right-hand side when they split a match below them, instead of inlining their values into each equation. Inlining duplicated the values, exponentially for nested haves, and lost the sharing of the join points that do notation introduces, which vcgen +jp [f] needs (#16122). The generated equations are otherwise the same: inlining the haves gives back the previous equations.* #16252\nfixes a kernel error unknown constant 'f.match_1.eq_1' when generating equation lemmas whose statements contain cast proofs, as happens when a match on a literal is reduced under a dependent argument. Definitions whose body eliminates an empty type now get one equation lemma stating the unfolded definition, instead of none.* #16219\nfixes timeouts when comparing distinct Float/Float32 literals, for example while generating the equation lemmas of a match on float literals. Unifying such literals used to unfold the float model on both sides in lockstep.* #16215\nfixes match on Float and Float32 literal patterns treating literals that denote the same value (such as 5 and 5.0, or 16777216 and 16777217 as Float32) as different patterns, which could cause a matching alternative to be skipped. Such overlapping alternatives are now also reported as redundant.* #16208\nmakes the equation lemmas f.eq_<i> keep the haves and lets at the root of the right-hand side, unless they must be zeta-reduced to split a match. For a definition without a match, f.eq_1 is now the same as f.eq_def.* #16138\nmakes Kernel.Environment.replay add the constants used in the types of all members of a mutual inductive block before adding the block. Previously only the dependencies of the member through which replay reached the block, and those of the constructors, were added, so a valid export could be rejected with unknown constant, depending on hash order.* #16132\nspeeds up type class resolution for classes with many instances.* #16105\nports the macro used for long list to a term elaborator instead. The term elaborator mimicks the logic of the previous macro but executes it noticeably faster than before.* #16091\navoids using an mvar for the type of a numlit when the type is known.* #16078\nimproves the performance of elaborating long list literals.* #16047\nfixes equation lemmas of structurally recursive functions whose rfl proof was very expensive for the kernel to check. For example, generating f.eq_2 for the following definition failed with kernel deep recursion, and cbv, rw [f.eq_2], and simp [f] failed with it:def f : Nat → Nat → Nat\n  | 0, x => x\n  | n+1, x => f n ((x * 1103515245) % 5)\n* #16046\nfixes quadratic elaboration behavior for large string-literal equalities such as theorem t : s = String.ofList l := rfl.* #15959\nchanges the parser of return to allow the term after return to start on a later line.* #15968\nspeeds up Environment.hasUnsafe, which mkAuxTheorem runs on the type and value of every auxiliary theorem, such as each proof produced by grind. Imported constants are now read from the imported constant map, which is computed once per traversal. Before, findAsync? wrapped each of them in an AsyncConstantInfo, allocating the wrapper, two Tasks and a ConstantVal.* #15955\nadds the @[vcgen_simp] attribute, which collects Sym.simp theorems for the vcgen tactic.* #15951\nmakes normalization for the TC cache fail if a local instance is not a free variable.* #15855\nadds a mkInstanceNameOfType for determining the fully qualified name of an instance and makes delta deriving use it.* #15898\nmakes @[deprecated] on syntax, macro, elab, notation and the mixfix commands deprecate the declared syntax, like deprecated_syntax.* #15942\nspeeds up lookups of attribute and environment-extension data for imported declarations, such as reducibility status, tag attributes, parametric attributes and MapDeclarationExtension.find?. These lookups run very frequently during elaboration, and their results are unchanged.* #15921\nspeeds up Expr.consumeTypeAnnotations, which the elaborator calls on the expected type of each application argument.* #15920\nspeeds up Expr.cleanupAnnotations, which match_expr and let_expr call on the discriminant and after every argument they peel.* #15897\nensures that the rules for macros which are deprecated are themselves not linted for deprecated declarations.* #15806\nallows users of the deprecated_syntax command to optionally specify a syntax transformation to produce a suggested replacement that will then be available as a code action.* #15879\nmakes notations, macros and other checked quotations (``(…)) report deprecated constants where they are defined, in addition to every place they are used.* #15858\nchanges many places that erase free variables from the local instances to use the same functions, LocalInstance.erase and LocalInstance.eraseAll (a new function), and makes them not rely on the fact that fvar is a free variable. Local instances which are not free variables are useful e.g. for the new deriving framework (#15320) but should not be used in code that may revert or clear free variables.* #15863\nmakes the elaboration of terms with many postponed type class problems, such as long arithmetic expressions over numerals, take linear instead of quadratic time. For example, def bla : Nat := 2 ^ 3 * 8 + 2 ^ 3 * 8 + ... with 80 summands now spends 65 ms in type class inference instead of 2.95 s.* #15824\nfixes dsimp and simp unfolding a function defined by mutual structural recursion when a match around a recursive call to another function of the group does not reduce. The resulting proof was accepted by the elaborator but rejected by the kernel.* #15737\nfixes fun_cases and fun_induction for a match whose discriminant appears in the type of another variable in the goal.* #15813\nmakes the \"deprecated syntax\" linter report in places it previously missed.* #15371\nexpands the condition for which the major of a recursor can be eta-expanded. Namely, subsingleton structures such as And should be eta-expanded in this situation.* #15103\nensures is_def_eq_proof_irrel runs before reducing any potential proofs in is_def_eq_core rather than after. This is already the order in which things run in the elaborator, so no need for any changes there.* #15373\neta-expands partial constructor applications.* #15682\nfixes mkBindUnlessPure to only run the continuation once, making sure that using pure () doesn't elaborate everything afterwards twice.* #15740\nmakes name resolution treat auxiliary local declarations (recursive references to the definition being elaborated, let rec and where) like the global declarations they represent, for both more consistent semantics and faster lookups.* #15738\nfixes a (kernel) unknown constant error for structurally recursive definitions where a recursive call appears as a match discriminant and an alternative of that match also recurses. Such definitions are now compiled using structural recursion.* #15318\nadds a getEntriesWithKeys function to DiscrTree that can be used to get all entries inside of a DiscrTree that have a specific key sequence.* #15710\nfixes the functional induction and cases principles for functions that match with a named pattern on a literal or a constructor without arguments (e.g. n@5 or b@true), which were previously rejected by the kernel.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.37.0-rc1 (2026-11-18)","header":"Language","id":"/releases/v4.37.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___37___0-rc1-_LPAR_2026-11-18_RPAR_--Language"}});